ú»Shortly before ten o‚Äôclock the stillness of the air grew quite oppressive, and the silence was so marked that the bleating of a sheep inland or the barking of a dog in the town was distinctly heard, and the band on the pier, with its lively French air, was like a discord in the great harmony of nature‚Äôs silence. A little after midnight came a strange sound from over the sea, and high overhead the air began to carry a strange, faint hollow booming.
